WHAT TO DO
1. In each failed job's log excerpt, find the FIRST error -- not the last
   symptom. Later errors are often consequences of an earlier one.
2. Read that job's definition above.
3. Note which files the error points at; you cannot read them here.
4. Then classify the failure.

CLASSIFICATION -- exactly one of:
  code            the product code at this commit is wrong.
  test            tests or test fixtures are wrong while the product behaves as intended.
  flaky           nondeterministic: the same job on the same commit both passes and fails.
  infrastructure  the execution environment the job ran in failed or lacked something.
  configuration   the pipeline definition or the project's CI settings are wrong.
  unknown         the cause could not be determined; the summary says what was checked.
If more than one applies, choose the one where the remedy belongs and mention
the other in the summary. "unknown" is a correct outcome: if you cannot
determine the cause, say what you checked. Never invent a cause.

EVIDENCE -- 1 to 8 items. Each is EXACTLY ONE of:
  {"job":"<failed job name>","line":"<log line>"}
      a line copied VERBATIM from that job's log excerpt above: one line, at
      least 12 characters unless it is a whole line, no "...", no joining of
      lines, no rewording. gonk checks every quoted line against the excerpt
      and refuses your WHOLE answer if a single one does not match.
  {"job":"<failed job name>","fact":"<key>"}
      a fact gonk listed above, by the key it is labelled with:
      failure_reason, runner, tags, stage, allow_failure,
      duration, history, definition, pipeline_error.
      pipeline_error is about the pipeline itself and may omit "job".
"job" must be the name of a job listed under "Failed jobs", copied EXACTLY
as it appears between the quotes in that job's heading: the same characters,
spacing and case, nothing added or dropped. If any failed job has a log
excerpt, at least one item must quote a line.

FILES -- up to 10 repository-relative paths the remedy concerns (no leading
"/", no ".."). They are citations: gonk never writes them. Required for
code, test and configuration.

gonk retries nothing, cancels nothing and changes nothing. The remedy is
advice to a human: what to change, and where. summary and remedy: at most
1500 characters each.

OUTPUT. Emit exactly one batch as the LAST thing in your output, fenced
EXACTLY like this, the JSON on a SINGLE line:

GONK_BATCH_START
{"effects":[{"kind":"diagnosis","classification":"<class>","summary":"<what failed and why>","evidence":[{"job":"<failed job name>","line":"<verbatim log line>"},{"job":"<failed job name>","fact":"<key>"}],"remedy":"<what a human should change, and where>","files":["<repository-relative path>"]}]}
GONK_BATCH_END

THE KEYS, spelled EXACTLY as here. Any other key, or one misspelled, costs you
the whole batch:
- diagnosis: kind, classification, summary, evidence, remedy, files
- one evidence item: job, line, fact ("job", and exactly one of the other two)

Nothing after GONK_BATCH_END. A real line break inside a JSON string is
invalid and costs you the whole batch: write \n instead. Escape quotes and
backslashes copied from a log line (\" and \\); the line is compared after
JSON decoding, so escaping does not break the match.
